  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1105 PM CST MON NOV 16 2026\n\nA shortwave trough accompanying the southern branch of the jet\nstream continues to track eastward along the Gulf Coast,\ngenerating mid- to high-level cloudiness across southern counties\namidst a dry low-level airmass. Surface high pressure anchored\nacross the eastern CONUS maintains dry easterly flow across\nCentral Alabama. Dew points continue to follow a blend of raw\nmodel guidance, as NBM values remain up to 10 degrees too high in\nseveral locations. Ensemble guidance has shifted slightly\nwestward with the cutoff low forecast to linger near the\nnortheastern Gulf Coast.\n\nAs a result, east-southeast 850mb flow will favor Atlantic\nmoisture advection into southeastern counties within the 850-\n700mb layer toward sunrise. Weak isentropic upglide will maintain\novercast conditions and potential light showers/sprinkles in the\nfar southeast through Wednesday. PoPs increase slightly Wednesday\nnight into Thursday, with slight chance PoPs expanded in this\nupdate; further upward adjustments may be necessary in future\nforecast packages. QPF will remain light, with no widespread\nwetting rains expected over the 7-day period. Persistent cloud\ncover will limit diurnal ranges, holding high temperatures\nslightly below normal while keeping lows elevated across the\nsouth.\n\nLow-end PoPs linger into Saturday until the shortwave ejects\nnortheastward, lifting the coastal low along the Eastern\nSeaboard. A cold front approaches the area during the day Sunday\nwith models beginning to converge on similar timing. \n\n16",
